Swiftvero Express — Frontend Build Prompt (Premium Revision)
Phase 1: frontend only. For use in Google AI Studio or an equivalent AI build environment.
0. What Changed In This Revision
Three corrections, applied throughout:
1. No carrier disclosure, anywhere a customer can see it. The earlier build showed "DHL Express Worldwide Linehaul" directly on the service-tier comparison. That must never happen again — not on that screen, not anywhere public-facing.
2. A genuinely premium visual identity, replacing the light green-and-white system, which read as generic SaaS rather than a premium international shipping brand.
3. Frontend only for this phase. No real backend, database, payment processing, or carrier integration. Everything is simulated with realistic mock data, built the way a real system would be structured later.
Everything else from the previous build — the booking workflow, the transparency standard on weight verification, proactive stall detection, reviews, cashback, and the realistic Nigerian pricing detail (VAT, jet fuel surcharge, export manifest lodging) — is working well and should be preserved.
1. Role and Product Vision
Act as a senior frontend engineer, premium brand designer, and conversion-focused product designer.
Build a polished, mobile-first, production-quality frontend only for Swiftvero Express Ltd, a Nigerian logistics technology company coordinating international cargo, parcel, and express shipping from Nigeria to supported destinations abroad.
This is a real, interactive application, not a plan or static screenshots. Every navigation item, button, and form must work end to end using realistic simulated data and local state. Build it the way a real system would be structured — a clear separation between UI components and a mock "data/service" layer — so a real backend can be connected later without a rewrite. Do not build actual servers, databases, or third-party integrations in this phase.
Swiftvero Express should solve practical shipping problems:
• Customers should never need to repeatedly contact Swiftvero to understand the next step.
• Customers should never be charged an unexplained final amount after an initial estimate.
• Customers should be able to declare their goods accurately and track their shipment's progress.
• Customers should receive proactive, honest communication when a shipment has an overdue update or a confirmed problem.
• Customers should be able to reach support without hunting for the right page.
• Swiftvero should project precision and restraint — the feeling of a brand that ships valuable things for a living, not a generic logistics app.
Carrier confidentiality is a hard rule, not a style preference. Swiftvero works with established international carrier networks behind the scenes, but the carrier's name, logo, or any identifying detail must never appear anywhere a customer can see it — not on the homepage, not in the quote flow, not on the service-tier comparison, not on the tracking page, not in FAQs, not in the footer. Refer to service tiers only by Swiftvero's own branded names — "Priority Air Express," "Standard Air Courier" — and describe them only by what the customer actually cares about: transit window, price, pickup conditions, and restrictions. A line like "DHL Express Worldwide Linehaul" or "Consolidated Air Cargo Courier via [Carrier]" must never appear. Replace any such line with a short, carrier-neutral description — for example, "Fastest available international lane" or "Cost-optimized international lane, consolidated for efficiency." This applies without exception to every public and customer-facing screen in this build.
The brand's central promise: Swiftvero Express — Your world, connected.
Homepage headline: "International shipping from Nigeria. Handled with precision."
Supporting copy: "Book international parcel and cargo shipments with transparent pricing, verified measurements, and proactive updates — without chasing anyone for answers."
Primary CTA: Get a Shipping Quote. Secondary CTA: Track a Shipment.
Trust principle, which should shape the actual tracking and support screens, not just the marketing copy: "When your shipment needs attention, you shouldn't have to chase us for answers."
Do not make unsupported claims about guaranteed delivery, universal destination coverage, perfect reliability, or any named carrier partnership.
