What premium means here: generous whitespace — when in doubt, remove an element rather than add one. Hairline borders (1px, #E4DFD2), not heavy drop-shadows; reserve shadow for genuinely floating elements like modals. Custom, consistent-stroke line icons, never a mixed pack, never emoji as UI icons. Asymmetric, editorial rhythm on marketing pages, not identical centered card grids. Quiet 150–200ms transitions, never bouncy or playful. Specific, locally-grounded copy (real VAT/FIRS references, jet-fuel-surcharge language, export-manifest detail) reads as premium and trustworthy — keep that level of specificity throughout.
On any single screen: at most one bordered "hero" card, at most one small badge or label per section, never a card nested inside another card. If a screen needs more than one visually distinct panel to explain itself, simplify the content rather than adding another panel.
Avoid, explicitly — the "AI-generated look": identical rounded cards repeated edge to edge; pastel gradient blobs or floating 3D shapes; soft shadows on every card and button; pill-shaped buttons and badges on every element; perfectly symmetrical centered layout on every section; generic stock photography of people smiling with parcels; more than one accent colour competing in one view; decorative sparkline charts added for texture; a caption under every icon even when it's self-explanatory.
Logo: use the file I provide. If none is available yet, build a refined placeholder from the wordmark plus a simple geometric globe or compass mark — never a cartoon airplane or box icon — and make it easy to swap later.
Responsive: mobile-first from roughly 360–390px, smooth on tablets, laptops, and desktop. Touch-friendly controls, an easily reachable primary CTA, sensible form steps instead of long intimidating forms, visible tracking information without excessive scrolling, no horizontal scrolling, no dense desktop-style tables, no tiny text. File uploads, date pickers, address entry, payment review, and document display must all be genuinely usable on a phone — and checkout especially must feel short and simple, never like a long form.
Public Website
Header: logo, Services, How It Works, Track Shipment, Business Shipping, About Us, Contact, Sign In, Get a Quote — with an accessible mobile menu.
Hero: headline and copy as above. Restrained, high-quality visual of international transit — never a generic airline-advert look or stock photo cliche. If real reviews exist, show the genuine rating and count near the hero; if not yet, an honest line that reviews will appear once eligible shipments are completed. Never invent a number.
Quick quote entry: origin country/city, destination country/postal code, shipment type, parcel count, approximate weight/dimensions, pickup requirement, customer type. Guide to the next step; if a precise rate isn't available yet, collect the details and explain a verified quotation follows — never fabricate a live rate.
How it works: Create a shipment request → Declare contents → Schedule pickup (free) or use an approved drop-off point → Verification and measurement → Review and approve the final quote, with an estimated delivery date → Pay securely → Carrier booking confirmed → Track and receive updates.
Services: the live launch service is international shipping — parcels, documents, and eligible cargo on supported routes. Never imply every cargo type or destination is supported.
Trust and communication: itemized quotations, verified measurements, milestone updates, proactive investigation of overdue updates, support, secure identity verification, a clear claims process. Never claim a feature is live before the workflow exists.
Business shipping: targets SMEs, online sellers, exporters, importers, recurring shippers. CTA: Business Shipping Enquiry. No bulk/trade-desk workspace in this version.
Reviews section: genuine published reviews only — rating, date, service category, verified-shipment badge.
Footer: About, Contact, FAQs, Track Shipment, Business Shipping, Shipping Terms, Privacy Notice, KYC Notice, Prohibited and Restricted Goods Policy, Cancellation and Refund Policy, Claims and Liability Policy, Priority Shipping Terms, Cashback and Referral Terms, Optional Protection Terms, Complaints and Dispute Resolution, applicable regulatory information, and the real social handles and phone number I provide.
Services / How It Works / FAQs / About / Contact pages: services filtered by verified route, destination, size, weight, and eligibility — never listed just because a carrier offers it somewhere. FAQs cover booking, supported routes, weight/dimensions, pickup and drop-off (both free), prohibited goods, final pricing, Priority vs Standard, customs charges, tracking delays, identity verification, cashback/referrals, damage/loss/claims/cancellation/refunds (including the cancellation disclaimer below), shipment protection, and support. Contact form: name, email or verified phone, shipment reference where relevant, reason, message, optional file.
Customer Accounts and KYC
Account creation, sign-in, password reset, contact verification, session handling — all simulated. Minimal profile fields: legal name, contact details, address where required, customer type, verification status, notification preferences.
KYC states, all simulated: Not started, In progress, Submitted, Pending review, Verified, Additional information required, Rejected, Expired/re-verification required. A customer cannot reach checkout without completing the required step — enforce this in the UI flow. Never expose ID documents or numbers on the customer-facing tracking page.
The Booking Workflow
A real multistep flow, visible stepper, inline validation.
Step 1 — Route: origin, destination, sender and recipient contact details.
Step 2 — Contents declaration: description, category, quantity, declared value and currency, package count, weight, dimensions, packaging, purpose, customs information, optional documents. Show prohibited/restricted guidance before submission and require confirmation. A restricted item pauses for review rather than silently blocking.
Step 3 — Pickup or drop-off, both completely free. No fee anywhere, under any circumstance — do not display a collection charge, and do not frame free pickup as a limited campaign. Doorstep pickup: date, time window, address, access instructions. Hub drop-off: an approved physical location with immediate staff verification. Keep the existing weight/dimension transparency pattern — actual weight vs. volumetric weight (IATA formula: L×W×H÷5000) vs. chargeable weight — and the "Swiftvero Transparency Standard" message that measurements are reverified in the customer's presence, with an itemized approval notification before payment if they differ. This is core to the brand promise; keep it prominent.
Step 4 — Verification and pickup tracking: actual count, weight, dimensions, condition, photos, discrepancies, screening status, custody handover. Give pickup its own visible status on the customer dashboard, separate from the overall shipment timeline: Pickup Requested → Agent Assigned → Agent En Route → Picked Up (or Rescheduled/Failed, with a reason). Send a clear notification the moment pickup completes successfully — this is one of the proactive-communication moments the whole product is built around.
Step 5 — Verified quote: itemized breakdown (base linehaul, applicable surcharges, security/documentation, collection — always ₦0 — optional protection, subtotal, VAT, total), currency, quote timestamp and expiry, selected service, and a calculated estimated delivery date — not just a transit-day range — derived from the pickup date plus the tier's transit window, clearly labeled as an estimate, not a guarantee. Record the original estimate and verified figures separately; require renewed approval if the quote changes materially; never silently change an accepted quote.
Step 6 — Service selection: Standard and Priority/Express side by side where both exist. Show only Swiftvero's own tier name, price, estimated transit window and delivery date, cutoff conditions, restrictions, and refund terms. Never a carrier name, anywhere. If no Priority option exists for a route, say so and show the alternative.
Step 7 — Acceptance: final summary of route, declared goods, verified measurements, pickup arrangement, selected service, itemized charges, total. Require acceptance of the quote, service terms, and declaration; record the exact document version accepted.
Step 8 — Payment: simulated initiation, pending, success, failure, cancellation, refund states. Receipt issued once payment is confirmed in the simulation. Keep this step short — checkout should feel like the fastest part of the whole flow, not the slowest.
Step 9 — Carrier booking: simulated confirmation, a clearly-labelled sample tracking number. Never surface the underlying carrier identity anywhere customer-facing.
Step 10 — Tracking and delivery: a readable timeline distinguishing confirmed milestones from internal notes, the estimated delivery date kept visible and updated, delivery confirmation and proof where available, access to history, support, claims, and the final receipt.
Customer Dashboard
Overview, New Shipment, My Shipments, Track Shipment, Quotes, Payments and Receipts, Pickups (showing the pickup-specific tracker from Step 4), Addresses, Reviews, Cashback, Refer a Friend, Support, Profile and Security. A clean overview — active shipments, pending quotes, upcoming pickups, payments awaiting completion, items needing action, recent tracking events, support cases, cashback balance, referral status, recent reviews — not an overwhelming table. Genuinely usable on mobile.
Proactive Stall Detection
The central product differentiator. Model shipment stages (pickup assigned, awaiting pickup, verified, awaiting payment, carrier booking pending, origin processing, international transit, customs clearance, destination processing, out for delivery, delivery exception) with a simulated process that, past a configured threshold with no new event, sends a truthful customer message — never inventing a reason. Example when no cause is confirmed: "Your shipment hasn't received a new tracking update within the expected window. We're checking the latest available information and will update you as soon as we have confirmation." The customer can always see: last confirmed event and time, whether an update is overdue, any confirmed issue, what Swiftvero is doing, and a support contact.
WhatsApp, Reviews, Cashback and Referral
WhatsApp: entry points on relevant screens using a mock adapter — status enquiries, tracking-reference submission, identity check before disclosing anything private, ticket creation, human escalation. Use a configuration value for the number, never invent one; show an honest unavailable state if needed.
Reviews: invitation 24–48 hours after delivery; overall 1–5 rating plus sub-ratings for communication clarity, delivery speed vs. estimate, and condition on arrival; optional photos and written review. Only a customer linked to a completed shipment can submit one — a tracking number alone isn't proof. States: invitation pending, submitted, pending moderation, published, rejected, hidden, flagged. Public company responses allowed; never suppress a legitimate negative review. Real averages only once genuine reviews exist.
Cashback and referral: simulated real monetary cashback (never a withdrawable wallet), released after delivery, closure, and any refund window — calculated on the eligible economic portion, not carrier pass-through costs or tax. Referral code/link, bonus only after the referred customer's first qualifying shipment clears, with self-referral and duplicate-account protection modeled. All states simulated; no real money moves.
Optional Shipment Protection
An add-on at the quote stage: coverage description, eligible value, premium and currency, exclusions, coverage limit, a separate affirmative opt-in, never pre-selected. Never implies Swiftvero is an insurer. Show as "coming soon" until a real product exists.
Legal and Policy Pages
Simple, readable pages: Terms of Use, Privacy Notice, KYC and Identity Verification Notice, Prohibited and Restricted Goods Policy, Shipping and Carrier Service Terms (generic tier language only, no carrier names), Pricing and Quotation Policy, Claims and Liability Policy, Cashback and Referral Terms.
Cancellation and Refund Policy, including this disclaimer: "Once your shipment has arrived at a Swiftvero facility, cancelling it does not include returning or redelivering the goods to you — Swiftvero is not responsible for returning or delivering the goods back after a cancellation at this stage. Arranging collection of cancelled goods from our facility is the customer's responsibility." Surface this plainly as an explicit acknowledgment at the moment a customer cancels a shipment already picked up or dropped off — not buried in a long document.
Plain language throughout, not intimidating legal copy. The real legal content needs a Nigerian legal adviser's review before any real launch; this prompt doesn't replace that.
Business Enquiries, Support, and Prohibited Goods
Business shipping: a simple form — business name, contact person, email/phone, estimated monthly volume, typical weights, common routes, frequency, message — with a confirmation screen; no internal review workflow needed since there's no admin side in this build.
Support and claims: a simple categorized form (booking, payment, pickup, tracking, customs, delivery, damage, loss, refund, general), reference number, clear confirmation. Claims collect shipment reference, description, date, optional photos.
Prohibited goods: plain-language guidance during contents declaration with a required acknowledgment; note it's general guidance, not a final determination for every destination.
Notifications
UI for key customer moments — booking confirmed, pickup confirmed, pickup completed, quote ready with estimated delivery date, payment confirmed, tracking update, overdue-update notice, delivered, review invitation — sample content only, no real sending in this phase.
Frontend Delivery
Real, working code: organized components, responsive layouts for every page in scope, realistic hardcoded sample data, clear empty/loading/success/error states, simple validation with helpful error messages. Keep it simple — a clean, well-organized set of components with good sample data is enough; don't over-build a backend-ready architecture at the expense of shipping something working now.
Build Order
1. Design system — Fraunces and Inter, the deep green/ivory/brass palette, restrained density. Get this exactly right first.
2. Public website.
3. Registration, sign-in, KYC, simulated.
4. The full booking workflow, Steps 1–10, with realistic sample data — free pickup, pickup tracking, delivery-date estimation, no carrier names. This is the heart of the product.
5. Customer dashboard, reviews, cashback/referral, WhatsApp entry points.
6. Legal pages (with the cancellation disclaimer), business enquiries, support.
7. Walk the complete sample journey start to finish, mobile first, then desktop. No visible demo controls, toolbars, or scenario switchers anywhere in the result.
Final Standard
Judge every screen against one question: would a first-time customer, with no explanation, immediately know what to do next? If not, simplify it. Checkout specifically should feel like the easiest, fastest part of the whole site. Premium means restraint and clarity, not more panels or badges. No admin tooling, no scenario switcher, no visible demo controls, no carrier names, no Nigeria-only language — just a beautiful, simple, global shipping website that's effortless to use and effortless to check out on.
